  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain is expected through this afternoon. Enough\nrain to make a small dent in the drought, mostly Downeast, is \nlikely with some places getting around an inch of rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nModels have continued trending toward a more southern track for\nlow pressure later tonight into Monday. The heaviest rain \ntotals will likely occur along, and just north of, the low \ntrack where a swath of 1 of rain is likely. Locally greater \ntotals are possible. This swath of heavier rain is likely to be\njust off shore and only hitting the immediate coast. Rainfall \ntotals will steadily diminish northwestward across central and \nnorthern portions of the forecast area. Current indications are \nthat rain totals from west-central to northeast portions of the \nforecast area should fall below half an inch. The least rain \nwill occur across portions of the North Woods where rain totals\nwill likely be minimal. A few thunderstorms are also possible \nalong the Downeast coast and Gulf of Maine in advance of the \nsystem. This is a relatively fast moving system. Rain will persist\nthrough Monday morning, then diminish during the afternoon with\nthe low exiting across the Maritimes. No snow is expected with \nthis system.\n\nBehind the system Monday night into Tuesday, steep low level \nlapse rates and some CAPE will lead to some snow showers across \nthe north, as temperatures will have quickly fallen below\nfreezing Monday evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An active weather pattern is likely from late on \nThanksgiving into next weekend. Forecast confidence on any particular\nweather system is low, but travelers should continue to monitor\nthis period for potential travel disruptions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe general weather pattern from Thanksgiving through the weekend is \nfavorable for active weather. This will likely impact holiday \ntravel. \n\nUpr lvl system moving into the Sierra Nevadas at this time will \ncontinue to move east and deepen on the lee side of the Rockies \nTuesday afternoon. This will result in cyclogenesis off of the coast \nof Florida late Wednesday and then the system moves up the eastern \nseaboard on Thanksgiving day. \n\nModels consistently hold precipitation back until late Thursday \nevening. H8 temps looks to be well above 0C at the onset of pcpn but \nthe surface temps will be hanging right around the freezing mark. \nBased on the latest soundings and NBM/s probability of ptypes it \nappears that there could be some cold air damming setting up over \nfavored areas acrs central and nrn areas, leading to a wintry mix \nbefore switching over to all rain. It is hard to get more specific \nthan this as it is simply too early to know with such significant \nuncertainty at this time.\n\nIn summary, it is still early, but be aware of the potential for \nactive weather that could impact travel Thanksgiving evening through \nSunday. Continue to check back for updates.",
